<h2 id="conditionals">Conditionals</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A condition has no parentheses; a body always braces. Parentheses only group.</li>
  <li><code>if … else if … else</code> works as expected.</li>
  <li><code>unless cond { … }</code> runs when false, with no <code>else</code>; <code>unless not x</code> / <code>unless !x</code> are BYN1007 double negatives, so write <code>if x</code>.</li>
  <li><code>if never cond { … }</code> panics if the condition is true, in every build mode, stating a fact the program relies on.</li>
  <li><code>if unlikely cond { … }</code> hints a cold block; nothing else changes.</li>
  <li>Null checks are <code>is null</code> / <code>is not null</code>; after <code>is not null</code> the value is non-null.</li>
  <li><code>switch</code> has no fall-through; a section may end with <code>break</code> or not. Join labels with <code>or</code> (<code>case 1 or 2:</code>). No <code>goto case</code>.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-statements-conditionals"/>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = true
print("x") unless not a</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1007:</span> `unless` with a negated condition is a double negative
  --&gt; st-unless.byn:2:12

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print("x") unless not a
<span class="gut">    │</span>            <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^ `unless` already negates</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: use `if` with the condition as written
<span class="gut">    │</span> 2 │ print("x") if a
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │            ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: the <code>if unlikely</code> hint is specified but unchecked; <code>if let x = e</code> is deferred. <code>while let</code> exists.</note>

<h2 id="postfix">Postfix modifiers</h2>
<p>A single simple statement may end with <code>if</code>/<code>unless</code>: <code>return</code>, <code>fail</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, <code>panic</code>, one call, one assignment, <code>++</code>/<code>--</code>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The condition is evaluated first; the statement runs only if it holds.</li>
  <li>The modifier stays on the statement's line (BYN1042).</li>
  <li>No modifier on a block; modifiers do not chain.</li>
  <li><code>stmt unless not cond</code> is BYN1007; write <code>stmt if cond</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code class="byn">return if n &lt; 0
fail .outOfStock if line.qty &gt; stock
let user = users.find(id) ?? fail .unknownUser
print("slow") unless fast</code></pre>

<h2 id="loops">Loops</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>for i in 0..n</code> counts 0 to <code>n</code> exclusive; <code>0..=n</code> includes <code>n</code>.</li>
  <li><code>for x in xs</code> walks a list, span, array, a string's <code>runes</code>/<code>bytes</code>, or a map.</li>
  <li><code>for (key, value) in map</code> destructures a tuple in the head, the only such place.</li>
  <li><code>for x: T in xs</code> types the loop variable; <code>for T x in xs</code> is BYN1043.</li>
  <li><code>while cond</code> and <code>do { … } while cond</code> work as usual.</li>
  <li><code>while let x = e</code> loops while <code>e</code> (<code>T?</code>) is not null, binding <code>x</code>.</li>
  <li>A label precedes the loop (<code>outer: for …</code>); <code>break outer</code> / <code>continue outer</code> use it, the only exit from nested loops since <code>goto</code> does not exist (BYN1010).</li>
  <li><code>for try x in seq</code> / <code>while try cond</code> may fail; failures pass to the caller. A fallible sequence without <code>try</code> is BYN2221 with a fix.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-statements-loops"/>
<pre><code class="byn">goto done</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1010:</span> `goto` does not exist in Bayan
  --&gt; st-goto.byn:1:1

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> goto done
<span class="gut">    │</span> <span class="hl">^^^^ not a keyword in Bayan</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: label the loop (`outer: for x in xs { … }`) and use `break outer` to leave it early
<span class="gut">    │</span> 1 │ break done
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │ +++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: <code>for x in xs except pattern</code> (BYN2022), <code>xs.indexed()</code>, <code>let (a, b)</code> destructuring. Lazy queries and readers for <code>for try</code> are not in the specification's first version.</note>

<h2 id="defer">Cleanup: defer</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>defer stmt</code> runs when the scope ends, however it ends.</li>
  <li><code>defer failing stmt</code> runs only on a failing exit, only in a function with a <code>fails</code> clause (BYN2606).</li>
  <li><code>defer panicking stmt</code> runs only during a panic.</li>
  <li>Deferred statements run in reverse registration order; they cannot fail, so no <code>try</code>.</li>
  <li>A panic does not unwind; only <code>defer panicking</code> runs, not plain <code>defer</code> or <code>defer failing</code>.</li>
  <li>A <code>unique</code> value is cleaned up once, when its owner goes away; no <code>using</code>.</li>
  <li>A postfix <code>if</code> on a deferred statement is decided at registration, not exit.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-statements-defer"/>
<pre><code class="byn">void f() {
    defer failing print("x")
}
f()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2606:</span> `defer failing` in a function that cannot fail
  --&gt; st-deferfail.byn:2:5

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     defer failing print("x")
<span class="gut">    │</span>     <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: remove `failing` to run it on every exit, or add a `fails` clause to the function</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>Planned: <code>defer panicking</code> and the process-wide <code>Panic.handler</code> were not checked here.</note>

<h2 id="requires">Validation: requires</h2>
<p>On a type or field, <code>requires</code> is checked at construction, so invalid values cannot exist.</p>
<ul>
  <li>In a field's <code>requires</code>, <code>it</code> is the value checked; each line is its own rule.</li>
  <li>A distinct type takes one too: <code>type distinct Count = i32 requires it &gt; 0</code>.</li>
  <li>Construction (literal or call) can fail: write <code>try</code> and declare <code>fails ValidationError&lt;T&gt;</code> on the enclosing function; handle with <code>catch</code> or <code>catch switch { .invalid e =&gt; … }</code>.</li>
  <li>The error names its type, <code>ValidationError&lt;User&gt;</code>; bare <code>ValidationError</code> is BYN2023.</li>
  <li>Fields cannot be set one at a time (BYN3208); use <code>with</code>, which checks again.</li>
  <li>A type with <code>requires</code> has no default value.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-statements-requires"/>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Count = i32 requires it &gt; 0
let c = try Count(3) catch Count(1)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2605:</span> `catch` needs a call that can fail
  --&gt; st-tryscript.byn:2:13

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> let c = try Count(3) catch Count(1)
<span class="gut">    │</span>             <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^</span></pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<note>In a script, top-level <code>try Count(3)</code> is BYN2605 (no <code>fails</code> clause). Put construction in a function. The example ends at top-level <code>return</code>, so its last line never prints.</note>
<note>Planned: <code>requires it.matches(re)</code> (needs a regex library), <code>T.rules</code> as data, <code>T.Patch</code> / <code>complete()</code>, <code>at .field</code> on a struct-level rule, and decoders collecting every violation. The error has no message text.</note>

<h2 id="expects">Preconditions: expects</h2>
<p><code>expects</code> states a parameter relation. A violation is a bug and panics in every build mode with <code>contract violated: …</code>; a constant argument that violates it is a compile error (BYN2321). <code>requires</code> on a function is rejected for <code>expects</code> (BYN1063). An <code>expects</code> reading one parameter warns, suggesting a type with <code>requires</code> (BYN5011).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Type with <code>requires</code></th><th scope="col"><code>expects</code></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Validating data</td><td>A contract between programmers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>On violation</td><td>Fails, and the caller can recover</td><td>Panics</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Checked</td><td>Once, at construction</td><td>Every call</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 gap(i32 a, to i32 b) expects a &lt;= b =&gt; b - a</code></pre>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 gap(i32 a, to i32 b) expects a &lt;= b =&gt; b - a
print($"{gap(9, to: 5)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2321:</span> `9` violates `requires a &lt;= b` of `gap`
  --&gt; st-contract.byn:2:14

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print($"{gap(9, to: 5)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^ fails the contract</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: pass a value that meets `requires a &lt;= b`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>With a run-time value, a violation stops the program. Here the compiler cannot see values from a list. It is not an example, since examples must exit 0.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 gap(i32 a, to i32 b) expects a &lt;= b =&gt; b - a

print($"{gap(2, to: 5)}")
let xs: List&lt;i32&gt; = [9, 5]
print($"{gap(xs[0], to: xs[1])}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre>3
panic: contract violated: a &lt;= b
  at ex.byn:1</pre>
  <figcaption>The real output of this program. The first call prints 3. The second panics, and the process exits with code 134. The location is the line of the <code>expects</code> clause.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="provide">provide and context</h2>
<p><code>provide allocator: arena { … }</code> sets implicit context values for the block; <code>context.allocator</code> reads one back. The old <code>context (…) { }</code> is rejected with a fix to <code>provide</code> (BYN6006). Handler rules are on <a href="ref-effects.html#provide">Effects</a>.</p>

<h2 id="goto">goto</h2>
<p><code>goto</code> does not exist; the compiler suggests a labeled <code>break</code> (BYN1010). Typestate changes use <code>become</code> (see <a href="ref-types.html#states">Typestate</a>).</p>
